Group Functions Or Aggregate Function
---------------------------------------
	- Oracle Having Following Group Functions 
	 1. max()
	 2. min()
	 3. avg()
	 4. sum()
	 5. count(*)
	 6. count(columnname)

	- Generally group functions are used to work or operate with number values in table and retun single value 

 1. max():
 	- it return maximum number of values from a table column 

 	Q: write a query to display maximum salary from emp table 

 		select max(sal) from emp;

 			=============================
			SQL> select max(sal) from emp;

			  MAX(SAL)
			----------
			      5000

			SQL>
			================================

	Q: write a query to display maximum date from hiredate from emp table 

		select max(hiredate) from emp;

			===================================
			SQL> select max(hiredate) from emp;

			MAX(HIRED
			---------
			12-JAN-83

			SQL>
			================================

	Q: Display maximum ename value in emp table 

		select max(ename) from emp;

			===================================
			SQL> select max(ename) from emp;

			MAX(ENAME)
			----------
			WARD
			=================================
				- Why only ward names comes because 
				- The MAX function, when used on a string column like ENAME, returns the lexicographically greatest value (i.e., the string that would appear last in alphabetical order).
				- In the alphabet, W is one of the last letters, so WARD is considered the "greatest" or "maximum" string value among all the ENAME values.

		- If we want to full row of ward employee from emp table 

			SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE ENAME = (SELECT MAX(ENAME) FROM EMP);

				==================================================================					
				SQL> SELECT * FROM EMP WHERE ENAME = (SELECT MAX(ENAME) FROM EMP);

				     EMPNO ENAME      JOB              MGR HIREDATE         SAL       COMM     DEPTNO
				---------- ---------- --------- ---------- --------- ---------- ---------- ----------
				      7521 WARD       SALESMAN        7698 22-FEB-81       1250        500         30

				SQL>
				===========================================================================



 2. min():
 	- it returns a minimum value from a table or column 

 		select min(sal) from emp;
 			
 			output=>800

 		select min(hiredate) from emp;
 			
 			output=>17-DEC-80

 Note1.: In relational Databases we are not allowed to use group functions in where clause 


		===============================================
		SQL> select * from emp where sal=min(sal);
		select * from emp where sal=min(sal)
		                            *
		ERROR at line 1:
		ORA-00934: group function is not allowed here
		===================================================

 3. avg(): 
 	- it returns avg from number datatype  column only 
		=================================
		SQL> select avg(comm) from emp;

		 AVG(COMM)
		----------
		       550

		SQL>
		=====================================

 Note2: In Oracle By default all group functions ingnores Null vallues except Count(*), If we want count null values in  group functions the we must use nvl() function

		======================================= 	
		SQL> select avg(nvl(comm,0)) from emp;

		AVG(NVL(COMM,0))
		----------------
		      157.142857

		SQL> 	
		=======================================

 4. sum()
 	- it returns total number of dataype column 

    Q: write a query to display total salary from emp table 

		=============================    	
		SQL> select sum(sal) from emp ;

		  SUM(SAL)
		----------
		     29025
		=================================

 5. count(*)
 	- it counts number of rows from a table

		==================================	 	
		SQL> select count(*) from emp;

		  COUNT(*)
		----------
		        14

		SQL>
		======================================

 6. count(columnname)
 	-it counts number of rows for perticular column and it will avoid null value fom rows 

		================================== 	
		SQL> select count(comm) from emp;

		COUNT(COMM)
		-----------
		          4

		SQL>
		==================================

 Note3: If we want to count distint from table column then we can use distinct clause within count() function 


 		===========================================
		SQL> select count(distinct(deptno)) from emp;

		COUNT(DISTINCT(DEPTNO))
		-----------------------
		                      3

		SQL> 
		============================================

		